---
/**
 * Light/dark toggle.
 *
 * Markup only. The behaviour lives in the bootstrap script in BaseLayout.astro,
 * because switching the theme has to happen BEFORE first paint and a script
 * down here in the body would flash the wrong palette on every navigation.
 * Keeping both halves in one inline script also keeps the page at one script
 * tag rather than two.
 *
 * HIDDEN UNTIL JAVASCRIPT CONFIRMS IT WORKS. The `hidden` attribute is in the
 * server-rendered HTML and the bootstrap removes it. A control that silently
 * does nothing is worse than no control, and anyone with JS disabled still gets
 * a correct dark theme from `prefers-color-scheme` -- they simply cannot
 * override it, so they are not offered a switch that cannot move.
 *
 * The label is set by the bootstrap too, since it names the theme the button
 * switches TO and only the resolved theme knows which that is.
 *
 * THE BUTTON IS SIZED TO ITS WIDEST LABEL, NOT TO ITS CURRENT ONE. The label
 * alternates between "light" and "dark", and those are different widths, so
 * the button used to resize on every click and shove the rest of the header
 * row sideways -- a layout shift the user causes, on a sticky element, on
 * every page.
 *
 * It is done with an invisible sizer rather than a fixed width or a `ch`
 * measurement. A hard-coded width goes wrong the moment the label or the
 * tracking changes; `ch` is only exact while the text is genuinely monospaced,
 * and the mono face is deliberately NOT preloaded (see AGENTS.md), so early in
 * the page's life this renders in a proportional fallback where `ch` would
 * measure the wrong thing. Stacking both labels in one grid cell makes the
 * button as wide as the longer string under whatever font is actually
 * resolved, with no arithmetic to get stale.
 */
---

<button
  type="button"
  id="theme-toggle"
  hidden
  class="kicker cursor-pointer border border-line px-2 py-1 text-ink hover:text-accent-hover"
>
  <span class="grid">
    {/*
      Both spans occupy the same grid cell, so the cell takes the width of the
      wider one. `invisible` is visibility:hidden, which still reserves space --
      `hidden` would collapse it and defeat the whole point.
    */}
    <span class="col-start-1 row-start-1" data-theme-label>Theme</span>
    <span class="invisible col-start-1 row-start-1" aria-hidden="true">Light</span>
  </span>
</button>
